<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Colors in Our World</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Purple Flower.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write numbers up to 10 with guidance. Interactive: Write one-word answers to simple questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write numbers up to ten with teacher guidance to represent objects in drawings (e.g., drawing four flowers and writing "4"). Can respond to simple questions about objects or colors by writing one-word answers with teacher support (e.g., "What color is this?" Write "blue."). Can use simple drawings to communicate about objects and add one-word descriptions (e.g., drawing a green leaf and writing "leaf").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 20px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows large flashcards of various colored objects (e.g., a blue bird, a purple flower, an orange cat, a green leaf, a red house). The teacher asks, "What color is this?" and students respond chorally. Then, the teacher holds up a card with a number (e.g., "3") and asks students to hold up the same number of fingers. This activates prior knowledge of colors and numbers visually and kinesthetically.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a picture of "one red flower" and explicitly writes "1 red flower" on the board, demonstrating proper letter formation and spacing. The teacher then shows a picture of "two green leaves" and writes "2 green leaves," emphasizing the plural form and number. The teacher models the initial sound /l/ in "leaf" and "blue" while writing. The teacher then writes "What color is this?" and points to a blue sky, then writes "blue" as a response, showing how to answer one-word questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a blue flower or a red flower?" (Pointing to a purple flower picture). Students point to "purple" or say "purple."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many cats are here: 1 or 2?" (Showing a picture of two cats). Students hold up 2 fingers or say "2."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards or a projected image showing simple drawings of objects with their written descriptions below them, e.g., "one orange cat," "two blue birds," "three green trees," "four red flowers." Each drawing clearly depicts the count, color, and object. A simple written dialogue asking "What color is it?" and responding with one word (e.g., "Blue.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Recognizing colors and objects: Identify familiar objects by color (e.g., "blue bird" or "red flower."). Describing with colors: Use simple color phrases in sentences (e.g., "That is a red bird."). Counting and identifying colors of objects: Identify and count familiar objects while specifying their color (e.g., "three blue birds" or "two red flowers").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a flashcard (e.g., "two blue birds"). Students trace the number "2" and the word "blue" in the air or on their desks.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a picture of a "purple flower" and asks, "What color is it?" Students write "purple" on mini whiteboards or a piece of paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays a drawing with "three green leaves." Students count the leaves and write the number "3" on their paper.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing numbers (1-10) and one-word color/object responses. Grammar: Color + noun ("green leaf"), WH-questions ("What color is it?"), Present simple ("It is an orange cat."). Vocabulary: bird, flower, cat, tree, house, leaf, sky, blue, purple, orange, green, red, tall, small, two, three, four.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice (Tracing and Copying):</b> Students receive a worksheet with dotted lines for numbers 1-10 and simple color words (red, blue, green, purple, orange). They trace each number and word. Below, there are simple pictures (e.g., a blue bird, a red flower) with incomplete sentences (e.g., "This is a ____ bird."). Students copy the correct color word to complete the sentence. (e.g., "This is a blue bird.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice (Drawing and Labeling):</b> The teacher shows a flashcard with a specific number of items and their color (e.g., "four orange cats"). Students draw the objects in their notebooks (e.g., four cats) and write the number "4" and the word "orange" next to their drawing. Repeat with "two green trees," "three purple flowers," and "one red house."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interactive Practice (Question & One-Word Answer Writing):</b> In pairs, students receive a set of picture cards (e.g., a blue sky, a red flower, a green leaf). Partner A asks, "What color is this?" pointing to a card. Partner B writes the one-word answer (e.g., "Blue." or "Red.") on a small whiteboard or paper. They switch roles after 2-3 turns. The teacher monitors, providing support for spelling and letter formation.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Colors in Our World".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual drawing and labeling activity followed by a guided writing task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students receive a blank sheet of paper and crayons. The teacher gives instructions for drawing and writing about "Colors in Our World," connecting to the theme "That's a Purple Flower."</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First, the teacher asks students to draw a specific number of objects of a certain color. For example, "Draw two red flowers" and "Draw three green leaves." As they draw, the teacher circulates, asking questions like "What color is your flower?" or "How many leaves do you have?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Next, students write the number and the color word next to each group of objects they drew (e.g., "2 red flowers", "3 green leaves"). They also draw a favorite object (e.g., a cat or a house) and write its color and name as a one-word description (e.g., "orange cat", "blue house").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, the teacher asks individuals to point to one of their drawings and describe it, encouraging them to say, for example, "That is an orange cat." or "These are two red flowers." They then point to their written words. This connects their writing to oral production and reinforces the pragmatic function of describing with colors and counting.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of student work (drawings with written descriptions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their drawing and labeling activity from Stage 4. The teacher will check the written numbers and one-word descriptions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in writing numbers (1-10) corresponding to the drawn objects.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and correct spelling of one-word color descriptions (e.g., "red," "blue," "green").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of object names (e.g., "flower," "leaf," "cat") as one-word labels.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effort and completion of the task.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a small paper slip with two questions:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write numbers 1 to 4?" (Students circle Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I write one color word?" (Students circle Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write today?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The writing activities for numbers and one-word answers were generally successful, especially with visual aids and tracing. Students enjoyed the drawing and labeling task, demonstrating good understanding of color-noun agreement and number representation. Some students struggled with letter formation for certain words (e.g., 'purple'). I noticed consistent errors with spelling 'orange.' Next time, I will provide more explicit letter formation practice for challenging words or use pre-printed word banks for reference during guided writing. More modeling of correct spacing between words is also needed. I should also integrate quick phonemic awareness checks during writing, asking "What sound do you hear at the beginning of 'flower'?" as they write the word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on writing simple descriptions. The next lesson (Mediation & Integration) will build on this by having students combine their written knowledge with oral communication and drawings to share information about colored objects with peers, potentially in a "Show and Tell" format, integrating all four skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>